    "raw_reply": "Looking at the three loops in this code, I need to analyze data dependencies carried between iterations.\n\n**Loop at line 7:** `for (int i = 0; i < LEN_1D; i++)`\n- Each iteration i reads `a[i]` and writes `a_in[i]`\n- Iteration i+1 reads `a[i+1]` and writes `a_in[i+1]`\n- Different indices, no dependencies between iterations\n\n**Loop at line 10:** `for (int i = 0; i < LEN_1D-1; i++)`\n- Each iteration i writes `a[i]`, `b[i]` and reads from indices i and i+1\n- Iteration i+1 writes `a[i+1]`, `b[i+1]` and reads from indices i+1 and i+2\n- Each iteration operates on different indices (a[i] vs a[i+1], etc.)\n- No dependencies between iterations\n\n**Loop at line 5:** `for (int nl = 0; nl < iterations; nl++)`\nThis is the complex case. In each iteration nl:\n- Line 8: Reads `a[0..LEN_1D-1]`\n- Line 11: Writes `a[0..LEN_1D-2]`\n- Line 12: Writes `b[0..LEN_1D-2]`\n- Line 13: Writes `a[0..LEN_1D-2]`\n\nIteration nl writes to global arrays `a` and `b`, which are then read and written by iteration nl+1:\n- RAW on `a`: nl writes at lines 11,13 \u2192 nl+1 reads at line 8\n- RAW on `b`: nl writes at line 12 \u2192 nl+1 reads at line 11  \n- WAR on `a`: nl reads at line 8 \u2192 nl+1 writes at line 11\n- WAR on `b`: nl reads at line 11 \u2192 nl+1 writes at line 12\n- WAW on `a`: nl writes at line 11 \u2192 nl+1 writes at line 11\n- WAW on `b`: nl writes at line 12 \u2192 nl+1 writes at line 12\n\nLOOP 5 RAW a 11 8\nLOOP 5 RAW b 12 11\nLOOP 5 WAR a 8 11\nLOOP 5 WAR b 11 12\nLOOP 5 WAW a 11 11\nLOOP 5 WAW b 12 12\nLOOP 7 NONE\nLOOP 10 NONE",
